de is in neither cs.Voters nor cs.VotersOutgoing (node.go:400-432), it restores the channel from n.propc only in the branch that reacts to a change of r.lead with a leader known (node.go:343-380), and Node.Propose sends into that channel, so a nil channel makes the call wait for the caller's context or the node's done channel (node.go:514-551); the target's own comment calls this reset 'not very sound and likely has bugs' (node.go:409-411) and the only documented caveat is that proposals can be lost without notice (node.go:138-140). Execution side, receipt 8b3e88d4b4a641fa8ddc723ecc0ba56d still shows the control holding (parsed index 1: while node 2 is a plain member that knows leader 1, Propose returns no error in 0ms and the leader's log index advances to 2) and the witness violated (parsed index 4: after the removal and the re-add were applied, with the leader's configuration back to voters=(1 2), node 2 committed and applied 4 and still knowing leader 1 in term 1, Propose returns only when its 300ms deadline fires and the leader's log index stays at 4), and the four remaining comparisons are unknown only because the compared fields sit inside the events' node2_state object. What this update corrects is the applicability of that receipt: the probe's pump called the wrapper's Ready/Advance loop but omitted the application's persistence duty - it never wrote Ready.Entries or Ready.HardState to the wrapped node's storage, although the contract requires HardState, Entries and Snapshot to be on stable storage before the messages of the same Ready are sent (doc.go:75-78, node.go:58-80) - so the prefix is not one a conforming application produces. The fault is visible in the draft harness: node 2's last entry identity reads (0,0) while its commit index is 4, because Advance removes entries from the unstable log under the assumption that the application has written them, and the same fault is why the attempted leadership-change control lost its election (the vote request